Step 1: Install Docker

-----------------------------------------------------------
bash
curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sudo sh
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
newgrp docker          # applies the group in this shell (or log out and in)
docker version
--------------------------------------------------------------
You should see both a Client and a Server section. If you see “permission denied”, the group change hasn’t applied yet; log out and back in.

Step 2: Your first container
--------------------------------------
bash
docker run hello-world
--------------------------------------
Read the output. It explains the steps Docker took: contacted the daemon, pulled the image, ran it, and streamed the output.

Step 3: Run a web server in the background
--------------------------------------
bash
docker run -d --name web -p 8080:80 nginx

Flags: -d runs detached (background), --name names it, -p 8080:80 maps host port 8080 to container port 80.

bash
curl localhost:8080
--------------------------------------
You should get the “Welcome to nginx!” HTML.

Step 4: Inspect it
--------------------------------------
bash
docker ps                  # running containers
docker ps -a               # all, including stopped
docker logs web            # nginx access/error logs
docker logs -f web         # follow live (Ctrl+C to quit); curl again in another terminal
docker stats --no-stream   # CPU and memory use
--------------------------------------
Step 5: Get inside the container
-------------------------------------
bash
docker exec -it web bash

Inside, run:

bash
hostname
cat /etc/os-release
ls /
ps aux          # may say "command not found" in a slim image; that's fine
exit
------------------------------------------
Notice the container has its own hostname and filesystem, but it is not a full OS.

Step 6: Lifecycle
--------------------------------------------
bash
docker stop web
docker ps -a        # status: Exited
docker start web
docker rm -f web    # force-remove
--------------------------------------------
Step 7: Run an interactive Linux in a container
-------------------------------------------
bash
docker run -it --rm ubuntu bash
# inside:
apt update && apt install -y curl
curl -I https://example.com
exit

--rm deletes the container on exit. Run it again: curl is gone, because every container starts fresh from the image. Containers are disposable. Remember this; it is central to Kubernetes.
-----------------------------------------------
Step 8: Compare size and speed
----------------------------------------------
bash
docker images
time docker run --rm alpine echo "started"
------------------------------------------------
Alpine is about 5 MB and starts in well under a second.

Step 9: Clean up and save notes
----------------------------------------------
bash
docker rm -f $(docker ps -aq) 2>/dev/null
---------------------------------------------
Write a short note in your repo: the 10 commands you used and what each does.
